A parent-only solution is to use Flexbox:

```css
.parent {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

```html
<div class="parent">
  <img src="img/world.png">
</div>
```

This centers the image, or other direct children, horizontally and vertically. Using a class targets just the intended parent rather than every `div`.

Assistant A is correct: Flexbox on the parent satisfies the constraint. Its example uses a broad `div` selector, which could affect other divs, but the centering approach works. Assistant B incorrectly claims that parent-only centering is impossible; Flexbox or Grid can do it without styling the child. Its absolute-positioning example also styles the image and, without a transform, does not actually center it. **My final verdict is Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]**

